.TH PASSWD 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
passwd \- change a user's password
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B passwd
.RI [ username ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Change
.IR username 's
password (default: your own). Changing your own password still
requires your current one first; root can change anyone else's
without it. The new password is hashed with a freshly generated
SHA-512-crypt salt (real randomness from
.BR /dev/urandom ,
never a fixed or predictable one) and written to
.B /etc/shadow
in place of the old hash.
.SH DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN REAL PASSWD
No password-strength/complexity checking, no password-history/reuse
checking, no expiry-policy enforcement.
.SH SECURITY
.B This has not been independently security-reviewed.
Modifies
.B /etc/shadow
directly, under the same lock file real shadow-utils uses. Read
.B common::auth
and
.B common::accounts
in the source before installing this setuid anywhere that matters --
the same weight as
.BR su (1)/ sudo (1)'s
own SECURITY sections. Get it reviewed and test it in a disposable
VM first.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-version\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, non-zero on failed authentication, mismatched
confirmation, an unauthorized target user, or a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR useradd (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR mitos\-box (1)
